#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11183
msgid "`cir.try` (::cir::TryOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11185
msgid "_C++ try block_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11196
msgid ""
"Holds the lexical scope of `try {}`. Note that resources used on catch "
"clauses are usually allocated in the same parent as `cir.try`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11199
msgid ""
"`cleanup`: indicates that there are cleanups that must be performed when "
"exiting the try region via exception, even if the exception is not caught."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11203
msgid ""
"Each catch handler region and unwind region receives a `!cir.eh_token` "
"argument representing the inflight exception. The first operation in a catch "
"handler region must be a `cir.begin_catch` operation. This must be followed "
"by a `cir.cleanup.scope` operation, with the `cir.end_catch` operation in "
"its cleanup region. The catch handling code will be emitted into the body of "
"the cleanup scope. This ensures that all paths out of the catch handler will "
"execute the end_catch operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11211
msgid ""
"A `cir.try` operation may instead have a `filter` handler paired with an "
"`unexpected` handler, which together implement the dynamic exception "
"specification of the enclosing function. The `filter` handler lists the "
"permitted type info symbols and holds the path taken when the in-flight "
"exception is permitted, which is a `cir.resume` that continues unwinding to "
"the caller. The `unexpected` handler holds the path taken when the "
"specification is violated, which is a `cir.eh.unexpected`. An empty "
"permitted type list represents `throw()`, which permits nothing, so the "
"filter handler region holds a `cir.unreachable` instead. Like `unwind`, and "
"unlike a catch handler, neither region begins with `cir.begin_catch`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11222
msgid ""
"The two handlers must appear together, with the filter first, and must be "
"the only handlers on the operation. The try operation created for an "
"exception specification wraps the entire function body, and each try "
"statement written in the source becomes a separate `cir.try` operation "
"nested inside it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11250
msgid "Example of a `void f() throw(int)` exception specification:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11270
msgid ""
"<table>\n"
"<tr><th>Attribute</th><th>MLIR Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>\n"
"<tr><td><code>cleanup</code></td><td>::mlir::UnitAttr</td><td>unit "
"attribute</td></tr>\n"
"<tr><td><code>handler_types</code></td><td>::mlir::ArrayAttr</td><td>catch "
"all, unwind, eh filter, eh unexpected or global view array attribute</td></"
"tr>\n"
"</table>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11278
msgid "`cir.try_call` (::cir::TryCallOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11280
msgid "_Try_call operation_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11282
msgid ""
"Similar to `cir.call` but requires two destination blocks, one which is used "
"if the call returns without throwing an exception (the \"normal\" "
"destination) and another which is used if an exception is thrown (the "
"\"unwind\" destination)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11287
msgid "This operation is used only after the CFG flatterning pass."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:0
msgid "`normalDest`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:0
msgid "`unwindDest`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11353
msgid "`cir.try_throw` (::cir::TryThrowOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11355
msgid "_Throw an exception with an unwind destination_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11367
msgid ""
"Similar to `cir.throw` but acts as a terminator with two destination blocks: "
"a `normalDest` that should contain a `cir.unreachable` operation (since a "
"throw never returns) and an `unwindDest` that receives control when the "
"throw needs to unwind through an enclosing cleanup or catch handler. This is "
"the EH counterpart of `cir.throw`, analogous to how `cir.try_call` is the EH "
"counterpart of `cir.call`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11374
msgid ""
"Like `cir.throw`, the absence of operands means rethrow. With operands, it "
"carries the same exception pointer, type info, and optional destructor as "
"`cir.throw`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11378
msgid ""
"This operation is produced by the FlattenCFG pass for `cir.throw` operations "
"that appear inside a cleanup scope or try region. It is later lowered by the "
"EHABI lowering pass to a `cir.try_call` of `__cxa_throw` (or "
"`__cxa_rethrow`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11419
msgid "`cir.unreachable` (::cir::UnreachableOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11421
msgid "_Invoke immediate undefined behavior_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11429
msgid ""
"If the program control flow reaches a `cir.unreachable` operation, the "
"program exhibits undefined behavior immediately. This operation is useful in "
"cases where the unreachability of a program point needs to be explicitly "
"marked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11434
msgid ""
"The `implicit` keyword marks an unreachable that CIRGen synthesized because "
"control flows off the end of a non-void function body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11448
msgid "`cir.va_arg` (::cir::VAArgOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11450
msgid "_Fetches next variadic element as a given type_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11458
msgid ""
"The `cir.va_arg` operation models the C/C++ `va_arg` macro by reading the "
"next argument from an active variable argument list and producing it as a "
"value of a specified result type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11462
msgid ""
"The operand must be a pointer to the target's `va_list` representation. The "
"operation advances the `va_list` state as a side effect and returns the "
"fetched value as the result, whose type is chosen by the user of the "
"operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11467
msgid ""
"A `cir.va_arg` must only be used on a `va_list` that has been initialized "
"with `cir.va.start` and not yet finalized by `cir.va.end`. The semantics "
"(including alignment and promotion rules) follow the platform ABI; the "
"frontend is responsible for providing a `va_list` pointer that matches the "
"target representation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:0
msgid "`arg_list`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11501
msgid "`cir.va_copy` (::cir::VACopyOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11503
msgid "_Copied a variable argument list_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11511
msgid ""
"The `cir.copy` operation models the C/C++ va_copy macro. The variable "
"argument list passed as the `$src_list` is copied to an unitialized "
"`va_list` in the destination operand. The next argument that can be "
"extracted from the copied list is the same as the next argument in the "
"source list. The copied list must be destroyed with `va_end`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:0
msgid "`dst_list`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:0
msgid "`src_list`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11537
msgid "`cir.va_end` (::cir::VAEndOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11539
msgid "_Ends a variable argument list_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11547
msgid ""
"The `cir.va_end` operation models the C/C++ va_end macro by finalizing and "
"cleaning up a variable argument list previously initialized with "
"`cir.va_start`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11551 ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11594
msgid ""
"The operand must be a pointer to the target's `va_list` representation. This "
"operation has no results and produces its effect by mutating the storage "
"referenced by the pointer operand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11555
msgid ""
"`cir.va_end` must only be called after a matching `cir.va_start` on the same "
"`va_list` along all control-flow paths. After `cir.va_end`, the `va_list` is "
"invalid and must not be accessed unless reinitialized."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11559
msgid ""
"Lowering typically maps this to the LLVM intrinsic `llvm.va_end`, passing "
"the appropriately decayed pointer to the underlying `va_list` storage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11580
msgid "`cir.va_start` (::cir::VAStartOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11582
msgid "_Starts a variable argument list_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11590
msgid ""
"The cir.va_start operation models the C/C++ va_start macro by initializing a "
"variable argument list at the given va_list storage location."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11598
msgid ""
"Each `cir.va_start` must be paired with a corresponding `cir.va_end` on the "
"same logical `va_list` object along all control-flow paths. After "
"`cir.va_end`, the `va_list` must not be accessed unless reinitialized with "
"another `cir.va_start`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11603
msgid ""
"Lowering maps this to the LLVM intrinsic `llvm.va_start`, passing the "
"appropriately decayed pointer to the underlying `va_list` storage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:11624
